## CACHE WRITE PATTERNS
============================

Abhi tak humne mainly read path dekha.

## EXAMPLE:
-------------

GET /products/101
     ↓
 Controller 
     ↓
  Service
     ↓
   Cache
     ↓
Cache Hit?
 ├── YES → Return Data
 │
 └── NO
      ↓
    Database
      ↓
 Store in Cache
      ↓
 Return Data


Lekin real problem write ke time aati hai.

Suppose currently:

Database:

productId:101
price = ₹79,999

Aur Cache me bhi:

productId:101
price = ₹79,999

Ab product ka price update hota hai:

PUT /products/101

New Price = ₹69,999

Ab question:

Database aur Cache ko kis order me update karein?

Isi problem ko handle karne ke liye different Cache Write Patterns use kiye jaate hain.

----------------------------------
1. CACHE-ASIDE WITH INVALIDATION
----------------------------------

Cache-Aside pattern me application pehle Cache check karti hai.

Cache Hit  → Data directly Cache se return.
Cache Miss → Data Database se fetch karke Cache me store
             kiya jata hai, phir response return hota hai.

Jab data update hota hai, application pehle Database me data update karti hai.

Important point ye hai ki Database update ke baad Cache me
stored data ko directly latest value se update karne ke bajay,
commonly corresponding Cache entry ko delete / invalidate
kar diya jata hai.

Iske baad same data ke liye next request par Cache Miss hota hai.
Application Database se latest data fetch karke Cache ko dobara
populate kar deti hai.


